        ht: 0.95, // Haitian Creole / kreyòl ayisyen (ht) — a FRENCH-LEXIFIED CREOLE of Haiti (~12M), Latin (the phonemic IPN orthography, Pressoir-Faublas 1979). A creole. A greedy scan + the nasal-vowel rule: ⟨ou⟩→[u], ⟨ui⟩→[ɥi], ⟨ch⟩→[ʃ], ⟨è ò⟩→[ɛ ɔ]; the Haitian ⟨r⟩→[ɣ] (velar fricative; →[w] before a rounded vowel, ayeropò→ajewopɔ), ⟨j⟩→[ʒ], ⟨y⟩→[j], ⟨g⟩→[ɡ]. THE CORE — the NASAL VOWELS ⟨an en on⟩ (plain a/e/o + ⟨n⟩) → [ã ɛ̃ ɔ̃] when the ⟨n⟩ is syllable-final (nan→nã, senk→sɛ̃k, lang→lãɡ), oral before a TRUE vowel (Dominik→dominik), still nasal before a glide ⟨y w⟩ (anyen→ãjɛ̃); a doubled ⟨nn⟩→nasal+[n] (Enndyana→ɛ̃ndjana); ⟨i⟩ and ⟨ou⟩ do NOT nasalize (machin→maʃin, moun→mun). + geminate collapse (accoma→akoma). wikipron hat_latn_broad (HUMAN, 1691 headwords) — 97.7% FOLDED / 99.4% symbol (100% first pass). NOTE: the backbone STRIPS the nasal tilde (combining mark) → the nasal comparison is on the base vowel; the engine DOES emit ã ɛ̃ ɔ̃. FOLDED: the rhotic ɣ~ʁ~ɰ, rare loan nasals (õ→ɔ̃, ũ→u), stress (unmarked). Residual = variable ⟨r⟩ realization (coda-drop) + loan vowels/clusters. single source.
