        kl: 0.90, // Kalaallisut / West Greenlandic (kl) — Eskimo-Aleut (Inuit branch), ~56k (Greenland), the 1973 PHONEMIC Latin orthography. A near-1:1 scan (the orthography is highly phonemic — Finnish/Māori-tier). THREE-VOWEL system /a i u/ (the Inuit hallmark): a→[a], i→[i], u→[u]; ⟨e o⟩ are NOT phonemes — they are the LOWERED allophones of /i u/ the orthography writes before a uvular ⟨q r⟩, so ⟨e⟩→[i], ⟨o⟩→[u] (aaneq→aːniq, nanoq→nanuq). the UVULAR stop ⟨q⟩→[q] + the uvular fricative ⟨r⟩→[ʁ]; ⟨ng⟩→[ŋ], ⟨nng⟩→[ŋː]; a doubled letter → LENGTH (illu→ilːu, aallaat→aːlːaːt); loan ⟨b d⟩→[p t] (no voiced stops: Bolatta→pulatːa); ⟨ts⟩→[ts]. No stress (weight-based, unmarked). **wikipron kal_latn_broad (HUMAN, 1581) — 94.8% FOLDED / 98.5% symbol.** A clean, high-scoring bring-up (transparent orthography + single-dialect referee = a tight measurement, unlike the multi-dialect Celtic ones). Folds: the length NOTATION (the referee writes a long vowel/consonant as a DOUBLED segment; we emit [Vː]/[Cː] but the backbone strips ː → collapse the referee's doubling to a single), and ʁ~r (the coarse broad-referee rhotic). The ~6% residual is entirely the DEFERRED NARROW phonetic layer that leaked into a few "broad" entries — the uvular vowel-lowering (o→[ɔ], e→[ɜ]), regressive consonant ASSIMILATION (anarnaq→[anɑnnɑq]), and ⟨ll⟩→[ɬ] (alleroq→[aɬɬɜʁɔq]). single-source family (wikipron; kaikki Greenlandic same Wiktionary source) but a transparent orthography → low referee-error risk. Floor 0.90 (below the 94.8%). Deferred: the phonetic assimilation + uvular-lowering NARROW layer, numbers.
