        qu: 0.88, // Quechua / Runasimi (qu) — Southern Quechua (Cusco-Collao + Ayacucho), Latin, ~7-8M Quechuan. The standardised trilingual orthography: a 3-vowel system ⟨a i u⟩ and a THREE-WAY stop series written OVERTLY — plain ⟨p t k q ch⟩, aspirated with ⟨h⟩ (⟨ph th kh qh chh⟩→pʰ tʰ kʰ qʰ t͡ʃʰ), ejective with an apostrophe (⟨p' t' k' q' ch'⟩→pʼ tʼ kʼ qʼ t͡ʃʼ); uvular ⟨q⟩→[q], ⟨ll⟩→ʎ ⟨ñ⟩→ɲ ⟨r⟩→[ɾ] tap ⟨sh⟩→ʃ ⟨y⟩→j ⟨w⟩→w. A longest-match scan + regular PENULTIMATE stress (onset of the penult syllable). kaikki Quechua (English Wiktionary, HUMAN, 171 headwords after loan/affix-stub filtering) — 93.0% FOLDED / 97.6% symbol; 88.3% skeleton agreement with epitran quy-Latn (INDEPENDENT programmatic 2nd, fold-heavy). KEY DECISION: uvular vowel-LOWERING (/i u/→[e o] next to q) is NOT emitted — the two independent referees CONFLICT (epitran lowers qillqa→qeʎqa; the kaikki primary normalises to phonemic /i u/, even raising lowered spellings enqa→/inqa/), so per "corroborate ≥2, don't reflex-fix" we emit the phonemic 3-vowel norm (matching the primary + the official orthography) and fold epitran's lowering. Folds: [ɪ ʊ]→[i u] (Wiktionary phonetic laxing of /i u/), r→ɾ (tap notation), χ→q (coda allophone of /q/), syllable dots. Residual ~7% = Cusco lowered-spellings (o/e near q vs the referee's /u i/) + loans (karru←car) + pre-normalization Spanish-influenced spellings (⟨c g z⟩ for k/q/ch/s). Floor 0.88 (below the 93.0% measured). primary human + independent epitran 2nd.
